// THE INVOCATION-CONTRACT STAMP: what the generated steps of gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow
// promise about the SUBJECT'S OWN BINARY -- which verbs it is asked to run, with which flags, and what
// shape of output a step reads back -- hashed into one literal that is emitted into the workflow AND
// read back off the checked-out tree.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.instruction_and_subject_resolved_from_different_revisions,
// silent arm. A pull-request run takes the WORKFLOW TEXT from the merge ref and the gunbc BINARY from
// the PR head (when this stamp was written the lane's checkout ref was github.event.pull_request.head.sha;
// it is now github.sha, the merge ref that also supplies the workflow, so the lane pairs them by
// construction and the stamp remains the guard for any step that checks out a different ref). When the head lacks the newest part of an instruction, the older binary still
// ANSWERS the newer command: runs 39066871919, 39067193189, 39067690307 and 39069382384 (2026-10-17)
// executed the new reuse walk against a head predating the ancestor locator, printed the six-field
// counts shape with no ancestor=, and exited GREEN with reused=0. The step's only contract on that
// output was a shell exit code, so a stale verb answering a fresh command was indistinguishable from a
// current verb with nothing to reuse -- and reused=0 is a legitimate outcome, which is what made those
// greens so easy to read as fine. Nothing refused, because nothing was written down about what the step
// would accept as an answer.
//
// THIS IS THE WRITTEN-DOWN, AND IT IS A PREMISE, NOT A SYMPTOM CHECK. The stamp is deliberately NOT
// "the counts line must contain ancestor=": that is one per symptom, hard-codes one field name, passes
// every other shape drift, and must be rewritten each time the contract moves (§5 -- a check that would
// also pass the bad thing is not a check). The stamp is the declaration the generator ALREADY HOLDS
// when it emits the step: the step says "run this verb, with these flags, and read this field set
// back". Refusing when the checked-out tree cannot have produced that declaration refuses the SPLIT
// itself, whichever part of the instruction the head is missing.
//
// THE PREIMAGE, AND WHY IT IS THESE ROWS AND NOT THE WORKFLOW TEXT. DESIGN §4d and the stated law of
// gunbc.required_ci_contract_epoch: do NOT digest workflow text, or every unrelated commit to main
// forces every open PR to merge main. So the preimage is ONLY the atoms a generated step puts in the
// subject's argv or reads from its stdout, taken from rows the generator already consumes. A change to
// main that touches no invoked contract must NOT move the stamp; a change to an invoked verb, a flag it
// is handed, or a field a step reads MUST move it, because the stale-subject case is exactly the case
// where those moved.

// THE CONSUMER OF THIS VALUE, named rather than implied (DESIGN §3c: a value with no consumer is a
// value that will be deleted, or worse, kept and disagreed with). It is read in exactly three places,
// and each is a DIFFERENT reading, which is the whole point:
//   1. THE EMITTED WORKFLOW, .github/workflows/witnesses.yml, carries the value as the workflow-level
//      `env: GUNBC_COMPILER_GATE_CONTRACT_STAMP:` LITERAL, written by
//      gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_entry. That is the RUNNING
//      side -- what the instruction in force promised.
//   2. THE SAME FILE AT THE CHECKED-OUT REVISION, read by the guard step this module emits
//      (compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_script -> compiler_gate_contract_stamp_read_from), one parse
//      of .github/workflows/witnesses.yml out of the tree the lane actually built. That is the TREE side
//      -- what the subject's own revision declared. The comparison IS the guard; nothing else consumes
//      the pair, so the value cannot become a decoration someone disagrees with.
//   3. THE WITNESS test.claim.compiler_gate_contract_stamp_witness, which binds both sides: the emitted
//      literal against this derivation, the refusal text against these strings, and the step's POSITION
//      against the emitted job.
// There is deliberately NO fourth reader: not the epoch, not the heal gate, not any admission walk. A
// second authority reading the same name would be the drift this value exists to prevent.

// THE NAME, one place, so the emitter, the refusal, the consumer above and the witness cannot drift
// apart. Deliberately a sibling of, and not equal to, required_ci_contract_epoch_env_key: the two hash
// different preimages and answer different questions, and a shared key would let one silently stand in
// for the other.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_key: String = "GUNBC_COMPILER_GATE_CONTRACT_STAMP"

data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_provenance: String = "gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow compiler_gate_contract_stamp"

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- THE DECLARED CONTRACT
//
// THE ATOMS. Each entry is a promise the emitted steps make about the subject's argv or its stdout, and
// each is spelled by the row that GENERATES the step rather than re-typed here (§3). Adding a verb, a
// flag or a read-back field means adding its atom; omitting one leaves the stamp silent about a promise,
// which is precisely the gap that let four stale-verb runs pass.

// THE VERBS AND THE FLAGS THEY ARE HANDED, DERIVED FROM THE ONE DECLARATION. These used to be three
// hand-typed argv shapes, and they had already drifted from what the gate emits: one of them described a
// "plain walk" -- verb plus --compiler and nothing else -- that no generated step anywhere performs. A
// hand-written description of an invocation is exactly as trustworthy as its author's attention, and the
// attention went elsewhere. Each row below is rendered from a walk's declared argument list, so an
// option that exists is in the hash and an option that does not exist is not.
//
// `--reuse-from` is the flag whose ABSENCE was the loud form of this class (unknown option --reuse-from,
// exit 127 with an empty --compiler) and whose silent form is the four greens above: a head predating it
// still accepted the command. That is what a stamp over the invocation is for, and it only works if the
// stamp's own copy of the invocation is not a fourth hand copy.
fn compiler_gate_invocation_argv_rows() -> List<String> {
  // THE PRODUCERS, CALLED AS THINGS ARE. The rows below are produced from what the real walk builders
  // return RIGHT NOW, not from a description of them written here.
  compiler_gate_invocation_argv_rows_from(
    reuse_args: parse_verdict_reuse_args(),
    publish_args: parse_verdict_publish_args()
  )
}

// THE SAME ROWS, OVER ARGUMENTS SUPPLIED BY THE CALLER. This parameterisation exists for one reason: the
// discriminator has to prove that MUTATING A PRODUCER moves the stamp, and in a pure model "mutate" means
// handing the real row builder a producer's list with one thing changed. Doing that through this function
// means the mutated case travels the SAME production path as the real one -- same renderer, same row
// shape, same hash -- so the proof cannot be faked by appending a row that only the hash ever sees.
fn compiler_gate_invocation_argv_rows_from(
  reuse_args: List<ParseVerdictInvocationArg>,
  publish_args: List<ParseVerdictInvocationArg>
) -> List<String> {
  list_append(
    left: list_map(
      // EACH ROW IS THE PRODUCER'S OWN ARGUMENT LIST, rendered by the renderer that builds the command.
      // This used to hash a pair of option lists declared in v2.cli.parse_verdict_invocation, while the
      // command was rendered from the producers' lists -- so the two agreed by attention, and the
      // attention went elsewhere: deleting cli_option_reuse_from from parse_verdict_reuse_args changed the
      // emitted walk and left the stamp unmoved, which defeats the guard entirely. There is one list now,
      // read twice: once to build the command, once to certify it. Mutating a producer therefore moves
      // the stamp, which is what the discriminator asserts.
      xs: [reuse_args, publish_args],
      f: fn(args) {
        join(
          ["argv:", parse_verdict_invocation_contract_row(verb: cli_verb_parse_population, args: args)],
          ""
        )
      }
    ),
    right: list_map(
      xs: emitted_subject_build_rows(),
      f: fn(row) { join(["argv:test ", emitted_subject_build_label_text(row: row)], "") }
    )
  )
}

// THE OUTPUT SHAPE A STEP READS BACK. The reuse walk accepts whatever the verb prints provided it exits
// 0, so the field set IS the contract -- and it is the field set the four silent runs in
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.instruction_and_subject_resolved_from_different_revisions proved
// unenforced: they printed an OLD counts shape (no ancestor=) and exited green.
//
// THE ROWS COME OUT OF THE FORMATTER, not from a list maintained beside it. These used to be built from a
// label list in gunbc.parse_verdict_schema, which meant the stamp hashed a TRANSCRIPTION of the print:
// renaming or dropping a field in v2.cli.parse_verdict_reuse_counts_fields left this row set intact and
// the stamp unmoved, so the guard went on certifying a contract the verb no longer has. Both the printed
// line and these rows are now produced from the one declaration
// (v2.cli.parse_verdict_reuse.parse_verdict_reuse_counts_fields), so a field the verb prints is stamped
// and a field it stops printing is not. That is the binding the review asked for, made by shared
// production rather than by a comment asking the two to stay in sync.
data compiler_gate_invocation_output_rows: List<String> = compiler_gate_invocation_output_rows_from(
  field_names: parse_verdict_reuse_counts_field_names()
)

// THE SAME ROWS, OVER FIELD NAMES SUPPLIED BY THE CALLER, for the discriminator's renamed-field case.
// Callers pass what the formatter says it prints; the parameter exists so a change to the FORMATTER is
// what moves the stamp, rather than an edit to this file.
fn compiler_gate_invocation_output_rows_from(field_names: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  list_append(
    left: list_map(
      xs: field_names,
      f: fn(label) { concat("stdout:", parse_verdict_reuse_counts_prefix, " ", label) }
    ),
    right: ["stdout:ancestor-attribution-required"]
  )
}

// THE LOCATOR'S BOUND. The restore chain walks a declared first-parent window and records the ancestor
// it restored from; the counts line then names it. The window's width is contract, because a head that
// cannot walk as far cannot make the claim the step makes. Drawn from the same row the locator, the
// gated restore chain and the record step are all generated from, so the stamp cannot disagree with the
// steps it stamps.
fn compiler_gate_invocation_locator_rows() -> List<String> {
  list_map(
    xs: parse_verdict_restore_ancestor_ordinals,
    f: fn(ordinal) { join(["locator:first-parent-ordinal:", to_string(ordinal)], "") }
  )
}

fn compiler_gate_invocation_contract_rows() -> List<String> {
  compiler_gate_invocation_contract_rows_from(
    argv_rows: compiler_gate_invocation_argv_rows(),
    output_rows: compiler_gate_invocation_output_rows
  )
}

// THE CONTRACT'S WHOLE PREIMAGE, ASSEMBLED FROM WHATEVER THE PRODUCERS AND THE FORMATTER SAY. Every
// caller that wants "the contract as it actually is" calls compiler_gate_invocation_contract_rows; the
// parameterised form exists so a DISCRIMINATOR can push a mutated producer through the same assembly and
// the same fold. Two halves come from producers, one from the locator's own declared window -- never from
// a row typed here, which is how the previous version's "plain walk" row came to describe a walk that no
// generated step performs.
fn compiler_gate_invocation_contract_rows_from(
  argv_rows: List<String>,
  output_rows: List<String>
) -> List<String> {
  list_append(
    left: list_append(left: argv_rows, right: output_rows),
    right: compiler_gate_invocation_locator_rows()
  )
}

// CANONICAL ORDER, for the same reason the epoch sorts its lists: a row appended in a different order is
// not a changed contract, and a stamp that moved on reordering would be a stamp that lies about being
// moved by a real change.
fn compiler_gate_invocation_contract_canonical_rows() -> List<String> {
  compiler_gate_invocation_contract_canonical_rows_from(
    argv_rows: compiler_gate_invocation_argv_rows(),
    output_rows: compiler_gate_invocation_output_rows
  )
}

fn compiler_gate_invocation_contract_canonical_rows_from(
  argv_rows: List<String>,
  output_rows: List<String>
) -> List<String> {
  sort_by(compiler_gate_invocation_contract_rows_from(argv_rows: argv_rows, output_rows: output_rows), f => f)
}

fn compiler_gate_invocation_contract_material() -> String {
  join(compiler_gate_invocation_contract_canonical_rows(), "|")
}

// THE SAME THREE REFUSALS AND THE SAME HASH, OVER ANY ROW LIST. Without this parameterisation the only
// way to ask "what would the stamp be if the contract gained a field?" is to write a second derivation,
// and a second derivation is a second authority that can be wrong in private. The witness asks the real
// function about a real modified row list; nothing recomputes the hash by hand.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_derivation_from_rows(rows: List<String>) -> CompilerGateContractStampDerivation {
  // CANONICALISED HERE, AT THE HASH. Sorting is part of what makes a contract the same contract under a
  // reordering, so it belongs with the hashing rather than with each caller -- otherwise a caller that
  // forgot to sort would get a different stamp for an unchanged contract, and the reordering control in
  // the witness could not be expressed at all.
  let ordered = sort_by(rows, f => f)
  if length(ordered) == 0 {
    CompilerGateContractStampNotDerived {
      reason: "the invocation contract declares no rows, so nothing would be promised and nothing could be refused"
    }
  } else if fold(
    ordered,
    init: false,
    f: fn(any_empty, row) { any_empty || string_length(s: row) == 0 }
  ) {
    CompilerGateContractStampNotDerived {
      reason: "a declared contract row is empty, so two different contracts could hash alike"
    }
  } else if string_contains(s: join(ordered, "|"), pattern: "${{") {
    CompilerGateContractStampNotDerived {
      reason: "a contract row carries a GitHub expression, so the stamp would not be a literal and could not be compared"
    }
  } else {
    CompilerGateContractStampDerived {
      stamp: content_hash_of_value(value: join(ordered, "|"))
    }
  }
}

// THE STAMP RENDERED FROM ANY ROW LIST -- one step, through the same derivation, so a witness comparing
// two stamps is comparing two runs of THIS function and not of two functions.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_value_from_rows(rows: List<String>) -> String {
  match compiler_gate_contract_stamp_derivation_from_rows(rows: rows) {
    CompilerGateContractStampDerived { stamp: stamp } => serialize_content_hash(hash: stamp)
    CompilerGateContractStampNotDerived { reason: _ } => "refused"
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE GATE ITSELF REFUSES TO EMIT WITHOUT. The same facts are also test fn claims in
// test.claim.compiler_gate_contract_stamp_witness, which is where a reader expects them; those claims
// delegate HERE, and this module is called from the generation gate, so a false one stops witnesses.yml
// being emitted at all rather than sitting green in a lane that never evaluates a claim.
//
// THAT MOVEMENT IS THE POINT. One of these predicates previously lived only as a claim asserting the
// stamp was 64 characters long, while the stamp is 16 -- so the claim could only ever have been red, and
// it survived every heal on this branch. A check nobody executes is not a check; refusing to generate is
// the only enforcement this repo has that a PR run cannot skip.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// EVERY ROW IS A PROMPT ABOUT AN INVOCATION OR AN OUTPUT, and none is a line of the workflow. A row
// carrying workflow text would move the stamp on any reformatting of the emitted file, which is the
// preimage mistake that would make the stamp useless: it would move on merges that touch no contract.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_rows_are_invocation_prompts() -> Bool {
  all(
    compiler_gate_invocation_contract_canonical_rows(),
    row =>
      (string_contains(s: row, pattern: "argv:") || string_contains(s: row, pattern: "stdout:") || string_contains(s: row, pattern: "locator:"))
        && !string_contains(s: row, pattern: "runs-on")
        && !string_contains(s: row, pattern: "steps:")
        && !string_contains(s: row, pattern: "- name:")
        && !string_contains(s: row, pattern: "${{")
  )
}

// THE RENDERED STAMP IS THE ONE SUPPORTED FORM, at the width the emitted awk states -- and the width is
// read from the same data row the awk embeds, so the two cannot drift apart by someone editing one of
// them.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_literal_shape_holds() -> Bool {
  compiler_gate_contract_stamp_is_derived()
    && string_length(s: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_yaml_value()) == compiler_gate_contract_stamp_literal_length
    && lower_hex_16(value: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_yaml_value())
    && compiler_gate_contract_stamp_rows_are_invocation_prompts()
}

// THE PREIMAGE DISCRIMINATOR, AS A REAL BEFORE/AFTER. Three assertions, all through
// compiler_gate_contract_stamp_value_from_rows, i.e. the same function the gate hashes with:
//   - ADDING a field the walk is specified to print MOVES the stamp. This is the four silent runs turned
//     into a check: they printed six counts fields where seven are declared, and a contract holding the
//     field set could not have hashed those heads the same as this one.
//   - REORDERING the rows does NOT move it. That is the canonical-order promise made testable rather than
//     asserted; a hash that moved on a reorder would fire on every unrelated edit.
//   - A row carrying a GitHub expression is REFUSED, not hashed, so an unresolvable value can never be
//     certified.
// THE DISCRIMINATOR: DOES THE STAMP ACTUALLY FOLLOW THE THING IT CERTIFIES?
//
// WHY IT EXISTS AND WHY IT MUTATES PRODUCERS. A stamp is only worth having if the contract can be shown
// to move it, and the previous version of this predicate proved the wrong thing: it appended a row that
// nothing but the hash ever read ("stdout:the-field-nobody-prints-anymore"), so it demonstrated that the
// FOLD is sensitive to its input -- which is what content_hash_of_value is for -- without ever showing
// that the input is the INVOCATION. Deleting an option from the real producer left that test green. Each
// case below therefore changes what a PRODUCER or the FORMATTER returns and pushes it through the same
// row builders, the same assembly and the same fold the artifact uses.
//
// THE CASES, AND WHAT EACH ONE WOULD MISS:
//   - drop an option from the reuse walk's own argument list: the walk would lose --reuse-from and start
//     recompiling everything, and the guard would still certify the run. This is the review's own
//     counterexample, executed.
//   - rename a field in the counts print: the guard reads `reused=` and the verb would print something
//     else, while the contract still named the old field.
//   - CONTROL -- reorder the rows: canonicalisation sorts before folding, so ordering is deliberately NOT
//     contract. Without this case, a "the stamp moved!" assertion is meaningless: any change to anything
//     moves a hash, so only the pair (moves when contract changes, does NOT move when it does not) says
//     the preimage is the contract.
//   - expression -- a GitHub expression in a row: refused, so a stamp can never be built over text whose
//     value is decided at run time.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_preimage_discriminator_holds() -> Bool {
  let before = compiler_gate_contract_stamp_yaml_value()
  let real_argv = compiler_gate_invocation_argv_rows()
  let real_output = compiler_gate_invocation_output_rows

  // ### Dropping an option from the REUSE PRODUCER, as if the producer had lost cli_option_reuse_from.
  // Built by filtering the producer's own list, so what changes is a real argument list rather than a row
  // typed here -- and the walk built from it would genuinely no longer pass --reuse-from.
  let reuse_without_reuse_from = filter(
    parse_verdict_reuse_args(),
    arg => arg.option != cli_option_reuse_from
  )
  let dropped_option = compiler_gate_contract_stamp_value_from_rows(
    rows: compiler_gate_invocation_contract_canonical_rows_from(
      argv_rows: compiler_gate_invocation_argv_rows_from(
        reuse_args: reuse_without_reuse_from,
        publish_args: parse_verdict_publish_args()
      ),
      output_rows: real_output
    )
  )

  // ### Renaming a field the FORMATTER prints, leaving the rest of the shape alone.
  let renamed_field = compiler_gate_contract_stamp_value_from_rows(
    rows: compiler_gate_invocation_contract_canonical_rows_from(
      argv_rows: real_argv,
      output_rows: compiler_gate_invocation_output_rows_from(
        field_names: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_field_names_with_renamed_reused()
      )
    )
  )

  // ### Control: the SAME rows, assembled in a different order.
  let reordered = compiler_gate_contract_stamp_value_from_rows(
    rows: sort_by(compiler_gate_invocation_contract_rows(), f => f)
  )

  // ### A GitHub expression must never enter a stamped row.
  let polluted = compiler_gate_contract_stamp_value_from_rows(
    rows: list_append(left: compiler_gate_invocation_contract_canonical_rows(), right: ["argv:${{ secrets.X }}"])
  )

  return before != "refused"
    && string_length(s: before) == compiler_gate_contract_stamp_literal_length
    && dropped_option != before
    && renamed_field != before
    && reordered == before
    && polluted == "refused"
}

// THE FORMATTER'S FIELD NAMES WITH `reused` RENAMED. Derived from the formatter's own list rather than
// spelled, so the case says "if the verb renamed THIS field" and follows the verb, instead of pinning a
// name that could drift into a case that no longer describes a rename.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_field_names_with_renamed_reused() -> List<String> {
  list_map(
    xs: parse_verdict_reuse_counts_field_names(),
    f: fn(name) {
      if name == "reused" { "reused_count" } else { name }
    }
  )
}

// THE OUTPUT CONTRACT IS A POPULATION, NOT A SUBSET, AND THE TWO SIDES ARE TWO PRODUCTIONS.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT A LIST COMPARED WITH ITSELF. An earlier version built `expected` from
// parse_verdict_reuse_counts_field_names() and then asked whether the stamped rows matched it -- but the
// stamped rows are built from that same list, so the question could not be answered falsely and the
// predicate proved nothing while its name promised a bijection. Here the sides are different: the stamped
// rows come from the field DECLARATIONS, and the line comes from the verb's RENDERER over a probe value.
// What is asked is "does what the verb prints contain exactly what the contract says it prints", which
// either side can answer falsely -- and when it does, witnesses.yml cannot be emitted.
//
// THE CONJUNCTS:
//   - every declared field appears in the printed line in `name=` form, the form the guard's readers key
//     on. A field dropped from the print reddens this, and that is the recorded silent-green itself: four
//     runs printed the six-field shape (no ancestor=) under a seven-field contract and exited green.
//   - the printed line EQUALS the field rows joined by the renderer's own separator. If
//     v2.cli.parse_verdict_reuse_counts_text were re-hardcoded to print six fields while seven are
//     declared, the rows and the line differ here. (An earlier draft counted `=` characters with
//     v2.std.text.string_split instead; that could not even typecheck -- that function consumes
//     FreeMonoid<Char>, not the host String this module reads -- and it was dropped rather than made to
//     compile by inventing a conversion.)
//   - the stdout rows the stamp hashes stand in bijection with the declared fields plus the attribution
//     row, so the preimage covers the whole shape and not a subset someone chose. A subset check passes
//     when rows quietly drop out, which is how a contract field stops being certified without any red.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_output_population_is_exact() -> Bool {
  let names = parse_verdict_reuse_counts_field_names()
  let printed = parse_verdict_reuse_counts_probe_text()
  let all_declared_are_printed = all(
    names,
    n => string_contains(s: printed, pattern: concat(n, "="))
  )
  let rendered_from_rows = join(
    [parse_verdict_reuse_counts_prefix, " ", join(parse_verdict_reuse_counts_probe_field_texts(), " ")],
    ""
  )
  let declared = list_map(
    xs: names,
    f: fn(label) { join(["stdout:", parse_verdict_reuse_counts_prefix, " ", label], "") }
  )
  let expected = list_append(left: declared, right: ["stdout:ancestor-attribution-required"])
  let stdout_rows = filter(
    compiler_gate_invocation_contract_canonical_rows(),
    row => string_contains(s: row, pattern: "stdout:")
  )
  all_declared_are_printed
    && printed == rendered_from_rows
    && all(expected, e => any(stdout_rows, r => r == e))
    && all(stdout_rows, r => any(expected, e => e == r))
    && length(stdout_rows) == length(expected)
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- DERIVED OR REFUSE
//
// The value is a function of the declared rows and nothing else, so no author can pin a stamp that
// flatters a stale tree, and the refusal to produce one is typed rather than silent. Mirrors
// required_ci_contract_epoch_is_derived: a module that cannot state its own stamp must REFUSE to emit
// one, not emit a placeholder that would compare equal to itself forever.
type CompilerGateContractStampDerivation
  = CompilerGateContractStampDerived { stamp: ContentHash }
  | CompilerGateContractStampNotDerived { reason: String }

// THE DERIVATION THE GATE USES, over the rows the module actually declares. The three refusals and the
// hash live in compiler_gate_contract_stamp_derivation_from_rows, which this calls -- there is exactly
// one implementation of "hash a contract", so the witness that asks about a modified contract is asking
// the same function, not a copy that could have drifted.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_derivation() -> CompilerGateContractStampDerivation {
  compiler_gate_contract_stamp_derivation_from_rows(rows: compiler_gate_invocation_contract_canonical_rows())
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_is_derived() -> Bool {
  match compiler_gate_contract_stamp_derivation {
    CompilerGateContractStampDerived { stamp: _ } => true
    CompilerGateContractStampNotDerived { reason: _ } => false
  }
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_underived_reason() -> String {
  match compiler_gate_contract_stamp_derivation {
    CompilerGateContractStampDerived { stamp: _ } => ""
    CompilerGateContractStampNotDerived { reason: r } => r
  }
}

// THE LITERAL THAT GOES INTO THE EMITTED WORKFLOW. "refused" is a value that can never equal a content
// hash, so an underived stamp fails the tree comparison rather than passing it -- refuse, never warn.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_yaml_value() -> String {
  match compiler_gate_contract_stamp_derivation {
    CompilerGateContractStampDerived { stamp: stamp } => serialize_content_hash(hash: stamp)
    CompilerGateContractStampNotDerived { reason: _ } => "refused"
  }
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp() -> String {
  compiler_gate_contract_stamp_yaml_value()
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- THE TREE-SIDE READ
//
// ONE PARSE, TYPED ARMS, NO WARNING ARM AND NO OVERRIDE INPUT (§5). The RUNNING side of the comparison is
// not read here: it arrives as the workflow-level env literal the runner injected, which is the file
// GitHub actually executed. The TREE side is this file as it sits in the checked-out subject. When the
// two differ, the instruction executing was not generated from the tree it is judging -- which is the
// split, and the refusal names both stamps.
//
// WHY NOT THE TREE'S OWN gunbc TO PARSE IT. The suspect in this class is the tree's binary, so a read
// that runs it would have the accused vouch for itself. The read goes through the ingested document,
// structurally -- never a substring scan, which would match the key inside a shell command, a heredoc or
// a comment and answer about text rather than about the workflow's environment (the law
// gunbc.required_ci_epoch_observation states for the sibling reader).
//
// WHY NO "MALFORMED HEX" ARM. Once the member is a non-empty string there is no further decoding step
// that could refuse; an arm for it would be a permanently unreachable RED, which std.contract_identity
// forbids on ContractEpoch itself. The arms below are each reachable from a document a fixture can hand.
type CompilerGateContractStampReading
  = StampPresent { stamp: String }
  | StampAbsent
  | StampUnreadable { detail: String }
  | StampMismatch { running: String, tree: String }

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_workflow_path() -> FilePath {
  artifact_path(a: WitnessFloorYamlArtifact) as FilePath
}

fn compiler_gate_stamp_env_entries(entries: List<YamlKeyValue>) -> List<YamlKeyValue> {
  fold(
    entries,
    init: [],
    f: fn(acc, kv) {
      if kv.key == "env" {
        match kv.value {
          YamlMapping { entries: es } => es
          YamlNull => acc
          YamlBool { value: _ } => acc
          YamlInt { lexeme: _ } => acc
          YamlFloat { lexeme: _ } => acc
          YamlString { value: _ } => acc
          YamlSequence { elements: _ } => acc
        }
      } else {
        acc
      }
    }
  )
}


// THE KEY COMES TO US ALREADY DECODED, SO THE COMPARISON IS AGAINST THE BARE KEY -- AND THAT IS A
// FACT ABOUT THE INGEST, NOT AN ASSUMPTION. extdeps.languages.yaml.ingest routes a chunk that starts
// with a quote through yaml_quoted_key_entry, which decodes the key with yaml_quoted_scalar before it
// ever builds the entry, and refuses the line outright if a quoted key does not close and sit directly
// before `:`. So by the time this module sees a YamlKeyValue, `"KEY": deadbeef...` has arrived as the
// key KEY, and a filter on the decoded key already counts it as a member.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT A QUOTE-STRIPPING HELPER. An earlier version of this filter peeled a quote layer off
// the key itself, on the belief that the ingest preserves quote characters. That belief was wrong, and
// the wrongness is the interesting part: it made the model look like a second reader that had to be
// repaired, when in truth only the emitted awk was blind -- awk sees the raw bytes, the model sees the
// decoded entry, and the two readers have different inputs by construction. A strip would have added a
// second decoder where the first one is already correct, which is §6's second authority free to disagree
// (and it would have been a decoder that invents data, the exact move this module refuses on the value
// side). The asymmetry is what the awk had to be fixed for; see compiler_gate_contract_stamp_awk_program.
fn compiler_gate_stamp_matching_entries(entries: List<YamlKeyValue>) -> List<YamlKeyValue> {
  filter(entries, kv => trim(s: kv.key) == compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_key)
}

// THE ONE SUPPORTED SCALAR FORM, and it is NOT DEFINED HERE. `lower_hex_16` is the predicate that
// already backs the `Fnv1a64StructuralDigestHex` carrier in std.content_hash -- the type this stamp IS
// the rendering of. Re-stating the hex alphabet and the width here would be a second authority free to
// disagree in silence (DESIGN §6), and the disagreement would be invisible until a subject passed one
// reader and failed the other.
//
// WHY STRICTNESS IS THE POINT AND NOT POLISH. The first version deleted every non-hex character and took
// the first member, so `XYZ0acdbc124e13bfecXYZ` returned 0acdbc124e13bfec with exit 0 and so did
// `${{ '0acdbc124e13bfec' }}`. A subject could therefore PASS the guard while its committed workflow
// carried no contract stamp at all -- the silent arm of the failure mode this module exists to close,
// reopened by the reader meant to close it. Deleting characters is a decoder that invents data; this one
// does not.
// THE WIDTH THE EMITTED READER HAS TO STATE AS A NUMBER. awk carries no type system, so the width of the
// `lower_hex_16` carrier whose rendering this is must appear as a literal in the program it embeds. It is
// ONE data row, referenced by both the awk text and the refusal that names it, and
// `compiler_gate_contract_stamp_literal_width_matches_the_carrier` holds it against the predicate itself
// -- so a drift between the two is a failed claim, not a silent disagreement.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_literal_length: Int = 16

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_is_valid_literal(s: String) -> Bool {
  lower_hex_16(value: s)
}

// A SECOND STAMP MEMBER IS UNREADABLE, NOT "THE FIRST ONE WINS". A workflow that declares the stamp
// twice is not a workflow whose stamp can be trusted, and picking one by position would be a silent
// resolution of an ambiguity the check exists to police.
fn compiler_gate_stamp_from_entry(kvs: List<YamlKeyValue>) -> CompilerGateContractStampReading {
  match length(kvs) {
    0 => StampAbsent
    1 =>
      match first(kvs) {
        Present { value: kv } =>
          match kv.value {
            YamlString { value: s } =>
              if string_length(s: s) == 0 {
                StampUnreadable { detail: "the stamp member is the empty string" }
              } else if string_contains(s: s, pattern: "${{") {
                StampUnreadable { detail: "the stamp member is a GitHub expression, not a literal" }
              } else if compiler_gate_contract_stamp_is_valid_literal(s: trim(s: s)) {
                StampPresent { stamp: trim(s: s) }
              } else {
                StampUnreadable { detail: "the stamp member is not the one supported scalar form: sixteen lowercase hex digits" }
              }
            YamlNull => StampUnreadable { detail: "the stamp member is not a string" }
            YamlBool { value: _ } => StampUnreadable { detail: "the stamp member is not a string" }
            YamlInt { lexeme: _ } => StampUnreadable { detail: "the stamp member is not a string" }
            YamlFloat { lexeme: _ } => StampUnreadable { detail: "the stamp member is not a string" }
            YamlSequence { elements: _ } => StampUnreadable { detail: "the stamp member is not a string" }
            YamlMapping { entries: _ } => StampUnreadable { detail: "the stamp member is not a string" }
          }
        Absent => StampAbsent
      }
    _ => StampUnreadable { detail: "the workflow declares the contract stamp member more than once" }
  }
}

// THE PURE HALF: it takes source text and decides, so every refusal arm is authorable from a fixture.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_read_from(src: String) -> CompilerGateContractStampReading {
  match ingest_yaml_source(src: src) {
    YamlIngestRejected { line: l, reason: r } =>
      StampUnreadable { detail: join(["document rejected at line ", to_string(l), ": ", r], "") }
    IngestedYaml { value: doc } =>
      match doc {
        YamlMapping { entries: es } =>
          compiler_gate_stamp_from_entry(
            kvs: compiler_gate_stamp_matching_entries(
              entries: compiler_gate_stamp_env_entries(entries: es)
            )
          )
        YamlNull => StampUnreadable { detail: "the workflow document is not a mapping, so it declares no env block" }
        YamlBool { value: _ } => StampUnreadable { detail: "the workflow document is not a mapping, so it declares no env block" }
        YamlInt { lexeme: _ } => StampUnreadable { detail: "the workflow document is not a mapping, so it declares no env block" }
        YamlFloat { lexeme: _ } => StampUnreadable { detail: "the workflow document is not a mapping, so it declares no env block" }
        YamlString { value: _ } => StampUnreadable { detail: "the workflow document is not a mapping, so it declares no env block" }
        YamlSequence { elements: _ } => StampUnreadable { detail: "the workflow document is not a mapping, so it declares no env block" }
      }
  }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- THE VERDICT
//
// Four arms and no fifth. The RUNNING value is compared as read: the emitted workflow carries a literal,
// and a running value that is not a literal is a generation bug the derived-or-refuse half above already
// refuses at emit time -- so there is no second parse here to author, and no arm to fake.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_compare(
  running: String,
  tree: CompilerGateContractStampReading
) -> CompilerGateContractStampReading {
  match tree {
    StampPresent { stamp: tree_stamp } =>
      if tree_stamp == running {
        StampPresent { stamp: tree_stamp }
      } else {
        StampMismatch { running: running, tree: tree_stamp }
      }
    StampAbsent => StampAbsent
    StampUnreadable { detail: d } => StampUnreadable { detail: d }
    StampMismatch { running: r, tree: t } => StampMismatch { running: r, tree: t }
  }
}

// THE DIAGNOSTIC IS THE POINT OF THE CHECK. Every refusal names BOTH stamps and the one remedy, and is
// located by the step that prints it. There is no warning arm and no input that overrides it (§5).
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_refusal_line(verdict: CompilerGateContractStampReading) -> String {
  match verdict {
    StampPresent { stamp: _ } => ""
    StampAbsent => join(
      [
        "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp: StampAbsent. The checked-out subject declares no ",
        compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_key,
        " at ",
        compiler_gate_contract_stamp_workflow_path_as_string(),
        ", so its workflow predates the instruction this job is executing. ",
        compiler_gate_contract_stamp_provenance,
        ". Remedy: merge origin/main into this branch."
      ],
      ""
    )
    StampUnreadable { detail: d } => join(
      [
        "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp: StampUnreadable {",
        d,
        "}. The checked-out subject's ",
        compiler_gate_contract_stamp_workflow_path_as_string(),
        " does not carry a readable ",
        compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_key,
        ". Remedy: merge origin/main into this branch."
      ],
      ""
    )
    StampMismatch { running: running, tree: tree_stamp } => join(
      [
        "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp: StampMismatch \{running=",
        running,
        ", tree=",
        tree_stamp,
        "}. The workflow executing was generated from a DIFFERENT revision than the tree it is judging: ",
        "the instruction came from the merge ref and the ",
        compiler_gate_contract_stamp_provenance,
        "-stamped workflow in the checked-out tree says otherwise. ",
        "Remedy: merge origin/main into this branch so the tree carries the instruction being run."
      ],
      ""
    )
  }
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_workflow_path_as_string() -> String {
  compiler_gate_contract_stamp_workflow_path() as String
}

// THE ARMS THE GENERATED STEP ACTS ON, kept as named predicates so the witness can assert each one is
// reachable rather than taking the step's word for it.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_is_refusal(verdict: CompilerGateContractStampReading) -> Bool {
  match verdict {
    StampPresent { stamp: _ } => false
    StampAbsent => true
    StampUnreadable { detail: _ } => true
    StampMismatch { running: _, tree: _ } => true
  }
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- THE GENERATED STEP
//
// ONE STEP, PLACED BEFORE EVERY STEP THAT INVOKES THE TREE'S OWN BINARY, AND ONLY IN THE JOB THAT DOES.
// It runs no product and reads nothing but the checked-out file, so it can sit ahead of the toolchain
// install: the earliest possible place the split can be refused is the one right after the tree appears.
//
// WHY IT CANNOT ASK THE SUSPECT. The binary this lane would normally use is the one built FROM THIS TREE,
// so a check that ran it to validate this tree would have the accused vouch for itself -- and when the
// tree predates the instruction, that binary predates it too. So the comparison is done by the runner's
// own awk over the committed file, and the only thing this step trusts is the env literal GitHub injected
// from the workflow it actually executed.
//
// WHY THAT AWK IS SOUND, AND WHERE ITS EDGE IS. It matches the stamp key ONLY inside the TOP-LEVEL env
// block -- the block at column zero, with the key at two-space indent -- so an indented step-level `env:`
// and a command body that mentions the key cannot both answer for it. That mirrors the structural, never
// substring, law of gunbc.required_ci_epoch_observation, and gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_contract_stamp
// claims bind the same four arms over the same document through the yaml ingest. THE HONEST EDGE: this is
// a line match, not a YAML parse, and the two readers could in principle disagree about a document neither
// the emitter produces. What that could cost is examined where the arms are consumed -- a false PRESENT
// still refuses unless the value matches, and the only direction that could spare a stale tree is a false
// ABSENCE, which this file shape cannot produce for a tree that was generated with a stamp at all.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_step_name: String = "Refuse a subject older than the instruction running it"

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_awk_program() -> String {
  // THE COUNTER IS INITIALISED IN A BEGIN BLOCK, NOT AS A BARE RULE. `in_env = 0` on its own is a
  // PATTERNLESS RULE, which awk runs for EVERY input line -- so it resets the flag on every line and the
  // stamp is never found. The fold model over the ingested document cannot show this, because there is no
  // per-line semantics there to get wrong; it surfaced only by running the emitted program against a file
  // that SHOULD have matched and watching it exit 3.
  //
  // RULES ARE SEPARATED BY NEWLINES, NOT SEMICOLONS. mawk -- which is /usr/bin/awk on the fleet's
  // ubuntu arm64 runners -- refuses several semicolon-separated `if` rules on one line with
  // "syntax error at or near if". The failure mode of getting this wrong is the loud kind on a runner
  // (awk exits non-zero, so the step refuses as StampAbsent), but it refuses for the WRONG REASON, and a
  // gate that reports "your tree predates the instruction" when its own awk is broken is a gate nobody
  // can trust the next time it fires. The fold is therefore exercised against the real interpreter in
  // test.claim.witness.compiler_gate_contract_stamp, not only modelled.
  //
  // A MEMBER IS RECOGNISED WITHOUT REGARD TO SPELLING. The rule was once
  // `index($0, "  " key ": ") == 1` -- an exact two-space, bare-key, colon-space prefix -- which made the
  // duplicate refusal CONDITIONAL ON THE DUPLICATE BEING WELL SPELLED: a second member written `KEY:` with
  // nothing after the colon (no colon-space) or `"KEY": deadbeefcafe0123` (a quoted key) was invisible to
  // the counter, so a workflow carrying one valid member and one competing one exited 0 as paired. Both
  // counterexamples were executed against this program and passed it. Recognition is therefore positional
  // and spelling-blind: leading whitespace is stripped, then the line must start with one of exactly three
  // needles -- `KEY:`, `"KEY":`, `'KEY':` -- each built from the SAME key the model compares against.
  //
  // A QUOTED NEEDLE CARRIES BOTH QUOTES. The first version searched for `"KEY:` and matched nothing,
  // because a quoted key is written `"KEY":` -- the CLOSING quote sits between the key and the colon. That
  // version looked correct in review and was found only by running the emitted program under the fleet's
  // awk, which is the difference between reading a guard and executing one. Quote characters come from
  // character codes rather than literals because a literal single quote would close the shell's single
  // quoting around this program.
  //
  // WHY THE MODEL DOES NOT NEED THE SAME TREATMENT, and this is not an asymmetry to be worried about: the
  // two readers have DIFFERENT INPUTS. awk sees the raw bytes of the file; the model sees entries the YAML
  // ingest has already decoded, and that ingest unquotes a quoted key (yaml_quoted_key_entry ->
  // yaml_quoted_scalar) before the entry ever reaches this module. So the model's filter on the decoded
  // key already counted a quoted-key member, and adding a quote-strip there would have created a second
  // decoder where a correct one already runs -- §6's second authority, free to disagree in silence.
  //
  // THE COUNTER IS INCREMENTED BEFORE THE VALUE IS EXAMINED, so an ill-spelled or malformed second member
  // still registers as a second member. Reversing those two statements puts the defect straight back.
  //
  // OVER-RECOGNITION IS THE SAFE DIRECTION: an extra line that merely looks like the key becomes a refusal,
  // and a false refusal is loud and typed, whereas a false acceptance is this module's silent arm.
  //
  // IT CARRIES NO SINGLE QUOTE, because the shell assigns it in single quotes; a quote inside would
  // close the quoting and let the remainder of the line be re-parsed.
  //
  // IT DELETES NOTHING. The first version did `gsub(/[^0-9a-f]/, "", v)` and accepted any non-empty
  // result, which is a DECODER THAT INVENTS DATA: `XYZ0acdbc124e13bfecXYZ` decoded to the real stamp and
  // exited 0, and so did a GitHub expression wrapped around it. A PR whose committed workflow carried no
  // contract stamp at all could therefore pass the guard -- the silent arm of this very failure mode,
  // reopened by the reader built to close it. The value must match the one supported scalar form
  // (compiler_gate_contract_stamp_literal_length) with every character in place, or it is refused.
  //
  // WHY IT PRINTS A SENTINEL INSTEAD OF JUST EXITING NON-ZERO. The step captures stdout and tests `-z`,
  // because the node serializer has no `if !` form. If a refused form printed nothing, it would fall into
  // the SAME arm as a genuinely absent member and refuse with StampAbsent -- "your tree predates the
  // instruction", blamed on the branch when the truth is that its stamp member is malformed. The sentinel
  // makes the two distinguishable, and it cannot collide with a real stamp: it contains uppercase and
  // non-hex letters, so the strict rule above can never accept it as one.
  join(
    [
      "\nBEGIN { in_env = 0; seen = 0; bad = \"\"; dq = sprintf(\"%c\", 34); sq = sprintf(\"%c\", 39) }\n",
      "function lstrip(s) { gsub(/^[ \\t]+/, \"\", s); return s }\n",
      "function is_member(l,  t) {\n",
      "  t = lstrip(l)\n",
      "  if (index(t, key \":\") == 1) { return 1 }\n",
      // THE CLOSING QUOTE IS PART OF THE NEEDLE. A quoted key is written `\"KEY\":`, so the quote sits
      // BETWEEN the key and the colon; the first version of this function searched for `"KEY:` and matched
      // NOTHING, which is how a quoted-key second member got past it a second time -- the fix was written,
      // looked right, and was executed against a real awk to find it did not fire at all. Three needles:
      // bare, double-quoted (both quotes present), single-quoted (both quotes present).
      "  if (index(t, dq key dq \":\") == 1) { return 1 }\n",
      "  if (index(t, sq key sq \":\") == 1) { return 1 }\n",
      "  return 0\n",
      "}\n",
      // FIRST DEFECT WINS, which is why every assignment below is guarded on bad being empty. Without the
      // guard the order INSIDE the member rule decides the reported reason: a second member with an empty
      // value is simultaneously a duplicate and a malformed value, the duplicate assignment runs first and
      // the form assignment then overwrites it -- so the guard would blame the VALUE while the model says
      // the member was declared twice. A refusal that names the wrong defect is the failure this module
      // refuses everywhere else, and the two have different remedies: delete a member, or regenerate.
      "$0 ~ /^env:[[:space:]]*$/ { if (in_env && bad == \"\") { bad = \"duplicate\" }; in_env = 1; next }\n",
      "in_env && $0 ~ /^[^[:space:]]/ { in_env = 0 }\n",
      "in_env && is_member($0) {\n",
      "  if (seen && bad == \"\") { bad = \"duplicate\" }\n",
      "  v = substr($0, index($0, \":\") + 1)\n",
      "  gsub(/^[ \\t]+/, \"\", v)\n",
      "  gsub(/[ \\t]+$/, \"\", v)\n",
      // ONE MATCHING QUOTE PAIR AROUND THE VALUE IS NOT PART OF THE SCALAR. This is what the YAML reader
      // this module folds over does (extdeps.languages.yaml.ingest yaml_quoted_scalar -> single/double
      // quoted), so `KEY: '0acdbc124e13bfec'` declares the SAME stamp as `KEY: 0acdbc124e13bfec`. The
      // first version refused the quoted spelling, which is a FALSE REFUSAL -- and the case table had
      // baked that refusal in as a passing RED, which is how a wrong reader survives review: nobody asks
      // whether the document it refused was actually illegal. An unbalanced quote is not a quote pair and
      // is left alone, so it still fails the strict form below; `${{ 'x' }}` is not a matched pair either.
      "  if (length(v) >= 2 && ((substr(v, 1, 1) == dq && substr(v, length(v), 1) == dq) || (substr(v, 1, 1) == sq && substr(v, length(v), 1) == sq))) { v = substr(v, 2, length(v) - 2) }\n",
      "  if ((v !~ /^[0-9a-f]+$/ || length(v) != " + to_string(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_literal_length) + ") && bad == \"\") { bad = \"form\" }\n",
      "  seen = 1\n",
      "}\n",
      // TWO SENTINELS, BECAUSE THE TWO REFUSALS BLAME DIFFERENT THINGS. The model already separates them
      // (compiler_gate_stamp_from_entry: a length other than one says "declares the contract stamp member
      // more than once", a bad value says "is not the one supported scalar form"). Collapsing a duplicate
      // into "malformed" would make the emitted reader refuse with a reason the model does not give -- the
      // wrong-reason failure this module refuses everywhere else, and one a reader cannot act on: the
      // remedy for a malformed value is to regenerate the workflow, the remedy for a duplicate is to
      // delete a member. Neither sentinel can collide with a real stamp: both contain letters outside the
      // hex alphabet and neither is compiler_gate_contract_stamp_literal_length long.
      "END {\n",
      "  if (bad == \"duplicate\") { print \"" + compiler_gate_contract_stamp_duplicate_sentinel + "\"; exit 4 }\n",
      "  if (bad != \"\") { print \"" + compiler_gate_contract_stamp_unreadable_sentinel + "\"; exit 4 }\n",
      "  if (!seen) { exit 3 }\n",
      "  print v\n",
      "}\n"
    ],
    ""
  )
}

// THE GUARD IS A NODE TREE, NOT A STRING. RULING ON THIS CARRIER, as given: "build the step as
// nodes, not strings ... use that same route for the guard so no shell syntax is hand-joined.
// Inside the awk program, it is acceptable if it is a single typed argument rather than spliced
// text." So every STATEMENT here -- both tests, every printf, every exit, the two assignments --
// is built through v2.extdeps.languages.bash_build and rendered by v2.workflow.bash_emit, which
// is the route the memory guard at gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow and the
// toolchain isolation carrier at v2.workflow.ci_workflow_run_emit already take. No line of shell
// in this file is concatenated from string literals any more.
//
// THE ONE STRING THAT REMAINS IS THE AWK PROGRAM, and it is a single typed argument to one
// command word, exactly as the ruling permits. It stays a string on purpose: it is a program in
// ANOTHER LANGUAGE, and bash_build has no node kind for an awk rule. Modelling it as bash nodes
// would be the worse lie -- a bash tree cannot express `$0 ~ /^env:/`, so the attempt would
// produce a node that claims to mean something it does not. Its correctness is instead pinned
// two ways: the witness runs it against the real interpreter, and the fold in
// compiler_gate_stamp_from_entry is checked to agree with it on the same fixtures.
//
// WHY THE CAPTURE CHANGED SHAPE, which is the one place the conversion is not a re-spelling.
// It used to read `if ! STAMP_TREE=$(awk ...)`, and the serializer cannot build that:
// bash_fold_serialize_negation_inner admits or_else, and_then, pipe and a nested negation, and an
// ASSIGNMENT IS NOT AMONG THEM -- so the `!` form has no node representation at all. It is
// replaced by capture-with-`|| true` (an or_else node, which IS admitted, inside a cmdsubst
// word) followed by `[ -z "$STAMP_TREE" ]`. Those are the same test, and not by approximation:
// the program prints the stamp if and only if it exits 0 (the `print v` and the `exit` are the
// same statement, and every failure path reaches `exit 3` without printing), so "the capture
// failed" and "the capture printed nothing" pick out exactly the same runs. That equivalence is
// what makes the swap a refactoring rather than a behaviour change, and it is the property the
// executed RED re-checks after regeneration, not a claim made here.
//
// THE `-e` CONSTRAINT SURVIVES THE SWAP. GitHub's default run shell is `bash -e {0}`, under
// which a bare `VAR=$(failing cmd)` aborts the STEP at that line -- so the old form's `if !`
// existed to stop the run dying with a bare exit 1 and no diagnostic. `|| true` inside the
// command substitution discharges the same obligation: the assignment's status is true, so the
// step continues and the `[ -z ]` arm prints the typed refusal. Both forms were verified by
// running them under `bash -e -o pipefail`, which is the only way to tell them apart.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_statements() -> List<Node> {
  [
    bash_build_assign(
      name: "STAMP_RUNNING",
      // The GitHub expression is ONE literal word. The serializer leaves a literal alone inside
      // its single quotes, so Actions still expands it at run time -- the same route the fleet's
      // other node-built carriers take for expressions.
      value: bash_build_word_lit(text: concat(concat("${{ env.", compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_key), " }}"))
    ),
    // NO LEVER TO SOFTEN THIS. continue-on-error, ::warning:: and any inputs./env.GUNBC_ALLOW
    // read are refused by the witness below; the node grammar has no warning arm to reach for.
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "-z"), bash_build_word_var(name: "STAMP_RUNNING")]),
      then_stmts: [
        compiler_gate_contract_stamp_print_line(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_refusal_parts_no_env()),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ]
    ),
    bash_build_assign(
      name: "AWK_PROG",
      value: bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_awk_program())
    ),
    bash_build_assign(
      name: "STAMP_TREE",
      value: bash_build_word_cmdsubst(
        body: bash_build_or_else(
          left: bash_build_command_with_words(
            words: [
              bash_build_word_lit(text: "awk"),
              // TWO WORDS, NOT ONE. `awk -v key=NAME` is one option and its argument, and the
              // serializer quotes each word SEPARATELY, so a single concat word renders as
              // '-v key=' 'GUNBC_...' -- two argv entries whose first has a TRAILING SPACE. mawk
              // then reads the lvalue as " key" and dies: `awk: improper assignment: -v  key=...`.
              // The capture is empty, the -z arm fires, and the guard refuses a CORRECTLY STAMPED
              // tree with StampAbsent -- a gate blaming the branch for a fault in the gate, which
              // is the exact wrong-reason failure this module's own comments warn about. It was
              // caught only by running the positive control on the emitted bytes.
              bash_build_word_lit(text: "-v"),
              bash_build_word_lit(
                text: concat("key=", compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_key)
              ),
              // The variable word renders as "$AWK_PROG" on its own: the double quotes are SHELL
              // SYNTAX the serializer supplies, not characters of the program text, and quoting the
              // substitution is what keeps the multi-line program ONE argument.
              bash_build_word_var(name: "AWK_PROG"),
              bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_tree_file())
            ]
          ),
          right: bash_build_command_from_lits(lits: ["true"])
        )
      )
    ),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [bash_build_word_lit(text: "-z"), bash_build_word_var(name: "STAMP_TREE")]),
      then_stmts: [
        compiler_gate_contract_stamp_print_line(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_refusal_parts_absent()),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ]
    ),
    // A MALFORMED MEMBER IS NOT AN ABSENT ONE. The reader prints a sentinel rather than nothing when the
    // member exists but is not the supported scalar form, so STAMP_TREE is non-empty here and this arm
    // fires BEFORE the `!=` comparison below can mislabel a forged value as an ordinary mismatch. Each
    // refusal names the arm it actually took: a gate that blames the branch for a fault in the gate is a
    // gate nobody trusts the next time it fires, which is the lesson the fused `-v key=` defect taught.
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(
        words: [
          bash_build_word_var(name: "STAMP_TREE"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "=="),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_unreadable_sentinel)
        ]
      ),
      then_stmts: [
        compiler_gate_contract_stamp_print_line(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_refusal_parts_unreadable_tree()),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ]
    ),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(
        words: [
          bash_build_word_var(name: "STAMP_TREE"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "=="),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_duplicate_sentinel)
        ]
      ),
      then_stmts: [
        compiler_gate_contract_stamp_print_line(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_refusal_parts_duplicate_tree()),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ]
    ),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(
        words: [
          bash_build_word_var(name: "STAMP_TREE"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "!="),
          bash_build_word_var(name: "STAMP_RUNNING")
        ]
      ),
      then_stmts: [
        compiler_gate_contract_stamp_print_line(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_refusal_parts_mismatch()),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ]
    ),
    // THE PROCEEDING PATH SAYS SO. Silence and agreement used to be indistinguishable in the log,
    // which is how the silent arm got green four times; the positive control's own line is the
    // difference between "the guard ran and agreed" and "the guard did not run".
    bash_build_command_with_words(
      words: [
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "printf"),
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "gunbc contract stamp: paired running=%s tree=%s\\n"),
        bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [bash_build_word_var(name: "STAMP_RUNNING")]),
        bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [bash_build_word_var(name: "STAMP_TREE")])
      ]
    )
  ]
}

// THE PATH, as a node literal word -- one value used by BOTH the awk argument and the
// StampAbsent message, so the file the guard reads and the file it names cannot drift apart.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_tree_file() -> String {
  compiler_gate_contract_stamp_workflow_path() as String
}

// ONE printf PER REFUSAL, as a node: `printf '%s\n' <one word>`. The format and the message are
// separate WORDS, so the message cannot be re-parsed as a format; each refusal is its own word
// built from literal and variable PARTS, which is how a stamp value reaches the message without
// any unquoted expansion of a tree-influenced value anywhere in the step.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_print_line(parts: List<Node>) -> Node {
  bash_build_command_with_words(
    words: [
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "printf"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "%s\\n"),
      bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: parts)
    ]
  )
}

// THE REFUSAL MESSAGES AS NODE PARTS, not as quoted strings. A literal part is emitted
// single-quoted by the serializer and a variable part as "$NAME", and the parts concatenate into
// ONE word -- so the shell interpolates the two stamps and nothing else, and no message can gain
// an unquoted expansion by accident. These are the strings the witness pins, so a rewording that
// drops a stamp, the remedy, or the verdict name leaves it red.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_refusal_parts_no_env() -> List<Node> {
  [
    bash_build_word_lit(text: "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp: StampUnreadable \{the executing workflow carries no "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_key),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: " in its environment, so it was not generated with an invocation contract. "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_provenance),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: ". Remedy: merge origin/main into this branch.")
  ]
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_refusal_parts_absent() -> List<Node> {
  [
    bash_build_word_lit(text: "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp: StampAbsent. The checked-out subject declares no readable "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_key),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: " at "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_tree_file()),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: ", so its workflow predates the instruction this job is executing. "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_provenance),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: ". Remedy: merge origin/main into this branch.")
  ]
}

// THE SENTINEL THE READER PRINTS FOR A MEMBER THAT EXISTS BUT IS NOT THE SUPPORTED FORM. Uppercase and
// non-hex letters, so the strict rule above can never accept it as a stamp and it cannot be forged into
// one: to make the guard skip this arm you would have to write a member that IS sixteen lowercase hex
// digits, which is the thing the arm is there to distinguish from.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_unreadable_sentinel: String = "UNREADABLE"

// WHY IT IS NOT FOLDED INTO THE SENTINEL ABOVE. See the END-block comment in
// compiler_gate_contract_stamp_awk_program: the two refusals name different defects and carry different
// remedies, and the model already keeps them apart. A reader that merges them is a reader whose refusal
// text cannot be trusted to say what it found.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_duplicate_sentinel: String = "DUPLICATE"

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_refusal_parts_duplicate_tree() -> List<Node> {
  [
    bash_build_word_lit(text: "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp: StampUnreadable \{the subject declares the member more than once}. The checked-out subject declares "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_key),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: " at "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_tree_file()),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: " more than once, in any spelling: a second member with an empty value or a quoted key counts, because a competing member is a competing member whether or not it is well spelled. One workflow cannot make two claims about the contract it was generated under. "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_provenance),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: ". Remedy: leave exactly one, and regenerate through gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow.")
  ]
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_refusal_parts_unreadable_tree() -> List<Node> {
  [
    bash_build_word_lit(text: "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp: StampUnreadable \{tree member is malformed}. The checked-out subject declares "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_key),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: " at "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_tree_file()),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: " but its value is not the one supported scalar form -- "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: to_string(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_literal_length)),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: " lowercase hex digits, nothing added around it. A workflow text is not an instruction you can edit into agreement. "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_provenance),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: ". Remedy: regenerate it through gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow.")
  ]
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_refusal_parts_mismatch() -> List<Node> {
  [
    bash_build_word_lit(text: "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp: StampMismatch \{running="),
    bash_build_word_var(name: "STAMP_RUNNING"),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: ", tree="),
    bash_build_word_var(name: "STAMP_TREE"),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: "}. The workflow executing was generated from a DIFFERENT revision than the tree it is judging: the instruction came from the merge ref, and the "),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_provenance),
    bash_build_word_lit(text: "-stamped workflow committed in the checked-out tree says otherwise. Remedy: merge origin/main into this branch so the tree carries the instruction being run.")
  ]
}

// WHY THE TREE SIDE IS READ BY THE RUNNER AND NOT BY A TYPED READER -- asked directly in review
// 82001 ("the step scans the YAML with awk, a grep standing in for a parse"), and the answer is
// STRUCTURAL, not convenience. A typed reader DOES EXIST: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_read_from
// parses with ingest_yaml_source into YamlValue and refuses with a located CompilerGateContractStampReading,
// and it is what the witness runs. It cannot be what the guard runs, for two reasons that are the
// same reason:
//
//   1. THE GUARD RUNS BEFORE ANY BINARY EXISTS. It sits at index 1 of the job, immediately after the
//      fleet checkout and before toolchain_home_isolation_step, which is before the first step that
//      invokes the subject. Its whole job is to decide whether that binary may be built at all. A
//      typed reader would have to be built first, which inverts the order the guard exists to enforce.
//   2. THE ONLY BINARY THAT COULD READ THE TREE IS THE SUSPECT'S OWN. The single gunbc available at
//      that moment is one built FROM THE CHECKED-OUT TREE -- the very artifact whose provenance the
//      guard is testing. Asking it to certify its own tree is the circularity the class row records
//      (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.instruction_and_subject_resolved_from_different_revisions): the
//      instruction and the subject resolved from different revisions is precisely the state in which
//      you cannot trust a subject-side reader to report the tree honestly.
//
// AND THE HEAD UNDER SUSPICION CANNOT EVEN CARRY THE READER. The four measured silent-green heads
// (39066871919, 39067193189, 39067690307, 39069382384) predate this module; a pre-stamp head has no
// compiler_gate_contract_stamp.dag to compile, so a guard that depends on a tree-side reader is a
// guard that is absent exactly when it is needed. That is a fail-open in the shape of a fix.
//
// So a pre-build read of a committed workflow member, performed by code that is NOT the tree's, is
// structurally necessary here. What is NOT necessary is the medium, which is why the scaffold below
// carries a dissolution obligation and why the awk program is one typed string argument rather than
// spliced text: it is a value passed to a command node, not shell syntax joined from strings.
//
// The scan's edges are stated rather than assumed, and the witness pins them: the awk program is a
// BEGIN-only program (no implicit per-line rule), it accepts the stamp only from a mapping key at
// column zero whose name is exactly the env key, directly under a column-zero `env:` -- so a step-level
// env of the same name cannot satisfy the read -- it strips non-hex characters before length/charset
// validation, and it prints if and only if it exits 0, with EVERY failure path reaching exit 3 and END
// exiting 3 unless a stamp was found. Reading it as a grep standing in for a parse would be right if
// it accepted a match anywhere in the document; it does not, and the controls prove the difference
// (a step-level env of the right shape reads StampAbsent, not StampPresent).
//
// DISSOLUTION: THE TRIGGER IS A CAPABILITY, NOT A FILE OR A DATE. This carrier exists because
// nothing at run time can read the tree's own workflow stamp without executing tree code, and
// the thing under suspicion is the tree. It dissolves the day a pre-build reader of that stamp
// exists that does NOT run tree code -- GitHub's own `workflow_sha` on the check run, or a
// runner-side typed reader for a committed workflow member. Naming a capability is what makes
// the obligation closable; naming a date or a path would let it sit marked forever.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_shell_emit_scaffold: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_contract_stamp",
    decl_name: "compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_statements",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_shell_emit_dissolution_trigger: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "dissolve-on: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_statements -- bash-node carrier that reads the tree's own committed workflow stamp BEFORE any binary is built and refuses a subject older than the instruction running it; dissolves when a pre-build reader of a committed workflow member exists that does not run tree code (GitHub's own workflow_sha on the check run, or a runner-side typed reader). The awk program remains a single typed string argument until an awk-rule node grammar lands.")

// THE SCRIPT, rendered from the tree. Poison on rejection rather than a silent empty run: an
// empty run body is a step that passes, and a passing step that checks nothing is precisely the
// silent green this module was written to end. The gate predicate below is what keeps poison out
// of a committed artifact; the poison string is what makes a rejection LOUD if the gate is ever
// bypassed by a caller that forgets it.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_emit_refused_poison: String = "__GUNBC_ORCH_EMIT_REFUSED__ gunbc contract stamp guard emission was rejected by v2.workflow.bash_emit"

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_script_outcome() -> Outcome<String> {
  match bash_emit_stmts(statements: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_statements(), set_e: false) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: ds } => Rejected { diagnostics: ds }
    Accepted { value: body, diagnostics: ds } =>
      Accepted {
        value: concat(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_marker_line(), body),
        diagnostics: ds
      }
  }
}

// THE MARKER, on the first line of the emitted script, in the same form the fleet's other
// shell carriers use: "# " followed by the dissolution description, which already begins
// "dissolve-on:". It is a COMMENT so it costs the runner nothing and cannot change behaviour.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_marker_line() -> String {
  concat("# ", concat(dissolution_description(condition: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_shell_emit_dissolution_trigger), "\n"))
}

// THE GATE PREDICATE the workflow generator consults before it will emit the step. It is a
// sibling of compiler_gate_contract_stamp_is_derived() and it fails closed: a node tree the
// serializer refuses stops the whole workflow from generating, rather than emitting a step that
// would silently check nothing.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_script_is_renderable() -> Bool {
  match compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_script_outcome() {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } =>
      // THE MARKER AND THE REFUSALS SURVIVE RENDERING. Checked here, in the path that produces
      // the committed artifact, and not only in the witness: a marker that a later refactor
      // drops would otherwise reopen exactly the unmarked-runner defect this carrier exists to close.
      !string_contains(s: script, pattern: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_emit_refused_poison)
        && string_contains(s: script, pattern: "dissolve-on: ")
        && string_contains(s: script, pattern: "exit 1")
        // THE READER'S OWN SHAPE, CHECKED WHERE IT CAN STOP THE ARTIFACT. These facts used to live in a
        // test fn named the_shell_fold_and_the_modelled_fold_agree_holds, which is deleted: with no host
        // exec at generation time it could never run the emitted reader, so it compared nothing and its
        // name promised a comparison it did not perform. Each check that was load-bearing in it is here
        // instead, because this predicate gates EMISSION -- a false one means witnesses.yml cannot be
        // generated, which is enforcement rather than a red test nobody evaluates on a PR run.
        && compiler_gate_contract_stamp_reader_program_is_well_formed()
        && compiler_gate_contract_stamp_model_reads_its_own_fixtures()
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE EMITTED READER, CHECKED AS A PROGRAM RATHER THAN AS A STRING SOMEONE READS. Each conjunct below
// closes a defect that was actually observed in this reader during review, and each is checked against
// the string that gets carried into the committed step.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_reader_program_is_well_formed() -> Bool {
  let program = compiler_gate_contract_stamp_awk_program()
  // THE PROGRAM IS CARRIED IN SINGLE QUOTES by the shell, so a single quote anywhere in it closes the
  // quoting and lets the rest of the line be re-parsed. An author editing the prose of a refusal can
  // reach this string, and the failure would be silent until a runner mis-parsed it. Quote characters in
  // the recogniser are therefore built with sprintf("%c", 34/39), never written.
  !string_contains(s: program, pattern: "'")
    // THE ENVIRONMENT FLAG IS INITIALISED IN A BEGIN BLOCK. A bare `in_env = 0` is a PATTERNLESS RULE,
    // which awk runs for EVERY line, so the flag is cleared on each line, no member is ever inside the
    // block, and a workflow that legitimately pairs exits 3 -- a refusal blaming the branch for a fault
    // in the gate. That exact bug was shipped once and is the wrong-reason refusal this module refuses.
    && string_contains(s: program, pattern: "BEGIN { in_env = 0")
    && !string_contains(s: program, pattern: "\nin_env = 0\n")
    // A MEMBER IS RECOGNISED THROUGH A FUNCTION, NOT AN EXACT PREFIX. The prefix form is what let a
    // second member hide: `index($0, "  " key ": ")` cannot see `KEY:` (no colon-space) or `"KEY": …`
    // (quoted key), so the duplicate refusal only fired when the duplicate was already well spelled.
    && string_contains(s: program, pattern: "function is_member(")
    && string_contains(s: program, pattern: "is_member($0)")
    // BOTH SENTINELS ARE EMITTED, so a duplicate refuses as a duplicate and a malformed value refuses
    // as malformed; one sentinel for both would make the emitted reader give a reason the model does not.
    && string_contains(s: program, pattern: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_duplicate_sentinel)
    && string_contains(s: program, pattern: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_unreadable_sentinel)
    // RULES ARE NEWLINE-SEPARATED: mawk (the fleet's /usr/bin/awk) rejects several semicolon-separated
    // if-rules written on one line.
    && string_contains(s: program, pattern: "\n")
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- THE FIXTURES, ONCE
//
// THE WORKFLOW SHAPE LIVES HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. It used to be spelled inside the witness module, which
// left two authorities over what "a workflow that pairs" looks like: the model's own reader was checked
// against one shape and the claims against another, and the two could drift with nothing to notice. The
// witness now imports these. THE SHAPE IS THE ONE THE EMITTER WRITES -- top-level env at column zero,
// member at two-space indent, single-quoted -- because a fold that only works on some other shape has to
// be caught here rather than on a runner. The surrounding name/on/permissions/jobs are not decoration:
// the fold walks the document, so a fixture carrying nothing but an env block would pass while a real
// workflow failed.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_workflow_with_env(extra: String, after_env: String) -> String {
  join(
    [
      "name: witnesses\n",
      "on:\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [main]\n",
      "permissions:\n  contents: read\n",
      "env:\n  CARGO_TERM_COLOR: always\n",
      extra,
      after_env,
      "jobs:\n  v2-bootstrap:\n    runs-on: [self-hosted, linux, arm64]\n",
      "    steps:\n      - name: Build the compiler\n        run: cargo build\n"
    ],
    ""
  )
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_after_env_marker() -> String {
  "PATHS: not-an-env-block\n"
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_stamped_env(stamp: String) -> String {
  join(["  ", compiler_gate_contract_stamp_env_key, ": '", stamp, "'\n"], "")
}

// THE STAMP A FIXTURE CARRIES IS THE CONTRACT'S OWN VALUE, not a typed constant. Both witness fixtures
// were hand-written 64-character strings while the stamp is 16 characters (Fnv1a64 via
// serialize_content_hash), so under the strict reader every one of them read as StampUnreadable -- and
// the claims that asserted a head "proceeds" were asserting it about documents the gate refuses. They
// stayed green because no lane on a PR run evaluates a claim; a reviewer running the reader found it. A
// value taken from the derivation cannot go stale the way a typed literal does.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_fixture_stamp() -> String {
  compiler_gate_contract_stamp_yaml_value()
}

// A SECOND VALID STAMP, derived rather than typed, for the mismatch and contract-changing cases. It comes
// from a DIFFERENT preimage row, so it is a different digest while remaining the one supported scalar
// form. If a future digest function ever made the two equal, the predicates that require them to differ
// go false and generation refuses -- which is loud, not silent.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_fixture_other_stamp() -> String {
  compiler_gate_contract_stamp_value_from_rows(rows: [compiler_gate_contract_stamp_fixture_preimage_marker_row()])
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_fixture_preimage_marker_row() -> String {
  "gunbc-fixture-other-stamp"
}

// A WORKFLOW THAT LEGITIMATELY PAIRS with the instruction that is running it.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_paired_fixture() -> String {
  compiler_gate_contract_stamp_workflow_with_env(
    extra: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_stamped_env(stamp: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_fixture_stamp()),
    after_env: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_after_env_marker()
  )
}

// THE SAME WORKFLOW, CLAIMING A DIFFERENT CONTRACT -- which is what a PR head that has not merged main
// does, and the shape the guard exists to refuse.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_stale_fixture() -> String {
  compiler_gate_contract_stamp_workflow_with_env(
    extra: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_stamped_env(stamp: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_fixture_other_stamp()),
    after_env: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_after_env_marker()
  )
}

// THE MODEL CLASSIFIES ITS OWN FIXTURES THE WAY IT CLAIMS TO. This is the half of the deleted parity
// claim that COULD be executed: the model's reader is model code, so its verdict on a document the model
// builds is decidable here. What it cannot establish is that the shell agrees -- that half is executed
// only by the enrolled evidence cases, which run the emitted bytes.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_model_reads_its_own_fixtures() -> Bool {
  match compiler_gate_contract_stamp_read_from(src: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_paired_fixture()) {
    StampPresent { stamp: read } =>
      if read != compiler_gate_contract_stamp_yaml_value() {
        false
      } else {
        match compiler_gate_contract_stamp_read_from(src: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_stale_fixture()) {
          StampPresent { stamp: other } => other != read
          _ => false
        }
      }
    _ => false
  }
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_script() -> String {
  match compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_script_outcome() {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } => script
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => compiler_gate_contract_stamp_emit_refused_poison
  }
}


fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_step_name },
    id: none,
    run: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_guard_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// ================================================================================================
// THE EVIDENCE IS ENROLLED, NOT ANECDOTAL.
//
// The case table -- which cases exist, which subject each reads, what exit code and which diagnostic
// each must produce -- is derived here, in .dag, and is the SINGLE AUTHORITY for it (section 3:
// interface, realization, policy). The emitted step carries those expectations INTO the required job,
// and the harness refuses locally if the committed runner's invocations have drifted from these rows.
// What remains in bash is the act of SPAWNING A PROCESS and reading its exit code and stdout inside a
// step that runs before any candidate binary exists; the residual and its two dissolution triggers are
// stated on the carrier itself, at test/compiler_gate_contract_stamp/run_evidence.sh, and nowhere else.
//
// THE SUBJECT IS A SYMBOL, NOT A PATH, on purpose. `fixture` / `committed` / `rewritten` / `demoted`
// name WHERE THE TREE COMES FROM; the runner binds each to a real file. A path in the model would be
// a second spelling of the same fact and could disagree with the runner in silence.
// ================================================================================================

// A VALUE, NOT A CHARACTER DELETION. The reader used to strip every non-hex character and take what was
// left, so a member that was never a stamp decoded into one and exited 0: `XYZ0acdbc124e13bfecXYZ`, and
// `${{ '0acdbc124e13bfec' }}` with it. A PR whose committed workflow carried no valid stamp could pass
// the gate built to catch it -- this module's own silent arm, reopened by the reader meant to close it.
//
// THESE ARE FORMS, NOT LITERAL VALUES, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Each is derived by the harness from the
// RESOLVED running stamp, exactly as SubjectRewritten is, so "the tree carries a stamp-shaped lie about
// the value the instruction actually runs" is a fact the harness enforces rather than a constant pair the
// model happens to agree on. They are enumerated here because the MODEL owns which forms exist and what
// each must prove. They cannot be passed as bytes: two of them contain a single quote, and the shell
// serializer single-quotes literal words, so an argv byte string would be re-parsed rather than quoted.
type CompilerGateContractStampForgedForm
  = ForgedWrapped             // the valid stamp with characters added around it (the original escape)
  | ForgedExpression          // the valid stamp inside a GitHub expression (the second escape)
  | ForgedTruncated           // the valid stamp with its last digit dropped (15)
  | ForgedExtended            // the valid stamp with one extra digit (17)
  | ForgedUppercased          // the valid stamp uppercased -- hex, but not the renderer's output
  | ForgedDuplicate           // the member declared twice with the same value
  // THE TWO SPELLINGS OF A SECOND MEMBER THAT A PREFIX-MATCHING RECOGNISER LET THROUGH, and which were
  // executed against the previous program to show it exited 0 as paired. They are the same kind of defect
  // as the six above, one level earlier: those proved the reader must not INVENT a value, these prove it
  // must not MISS a member. A duplicate check that only fires when the duplicate is spelled exactly as the
  // check expects is a check on nothing -- the well-spelled case is the one that would have been caught by
  // any counter at all.
  | ForgedSecondEmpty         // a second member with NOTHING after the colon (no ": " to match)
  | ForgedSecondQuotedKey     // a second member whose KEY is quoted ("KEY": <valid>)

type CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceSubject
  = SubjectFixture            // the committed silent-green head's own witnesses.yml
  | SubjectCommitted          // the committed artifact, unchanged
  | SubjectRewritten          // its stamp member moved, as a contract-changing PR would
  | SubjectDemoted            // the member demoted into a step-level env, structurally absent
  // THE SAME VALID STAMP WRITTEN AS A QUOTED SCALAR, which is LEGAL. The YAML reader this module
  // folds over unquotes a quoted scalar (extdeps.languages.yaml.ingest yaml_quoted_scalar), so
  // `KEY: 0acdbc...` declares exactly the same stamp as `KEY: 0acdbc...` -- one matching quote
  // pair is not part of the scalar. This is a CONTROL, and it exists because the emitted reader
  // was once wrong in the OVER-REFUSING direction: it required the sixteen characters to sit bare
  // after the colon, refused the quoted spelling, and this table had baked that refusal in as a
  // PASSING RED named "forged quoted scalar". Refusing a legal document is not a safe error -- it
  // is the guard refusing PRs whose workflow is fine, and naming a defect that does not exist --
  // and it survived because a red-looking case is never questioned. Calling a quote "forged" was
  // the tell.
  | SubjectQuotedScalar
  | SubjectForged { form: CompilerGateContractStampForgedForm }   // member forged in exactly this way

type CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
  label: String
  subject: CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceSubject
  running: String             // the running-side value substituted for ${{ env.X }}; "" = absent
  expected_exit: String       // compared against the guard's exit status
  expected_finding: String    // a substring the diagnostic must contain
}

// THE FOUR MEASURED SILENT-GREEN RUNS share one committed workflow artifact, so ONE SubjectFixture
// row stands for all four; fixtures/README.md carries the run ids and each run's measurements. The
// expected finding is asserted as well as the exit code because a guard that dies for an unrelated
// reason also exits 1 -- that is exactly how the fused `-v key=` defect passed every RED case.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_cases() -> List<CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase> {
  [
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "RED silent-green head 5003782716",
      subject: SubjectFixture,
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampAbsent"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "CONTROL paired (head carries the stamp)",
      subject: SubjectCommitted,
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "0",
      expected_finding: "paired"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "CONTROL mismatch (running != tree)",
      subject: SubjectCommitted,
      running: "deadbeefcafe0123",
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampMismatch"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "CONTROL unreadable (no running stamp)",
      subject: SubjectCommitted,
      running: "",
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampUnreadable"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "CONTROL contract-changing PR (both new)",
      subject: SubjectRewritten,
      running: "0123456789abcdef",
      expected_exit: "0",
      expected_finding: "paired"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "CONTROL step-level env is not the read",
      subject: SubjectDemoted,
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampAbsent"
    },
    // THE FORGED FORMS. Each tree carries a value a permissive reader would decode into the running
    // stamp, so each is a RED against the specific escape the old gsub allowed -- and, unlike the
    // controls above, against a tree that WOULD otherwise pair. They run against the emitted guard's own
    // bytes, not against a shell re-implementation of it, so they exercise the awk that actually ships.
    // The values are built by the harness from the resolved running stamp (subject `forged:<form>`),
    // which keeps "the lie is about the value the instruction runs" enforced by construction.
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "RED forged wrapped (chars around the stamp)",
      subject: SubjectForged { form: ForgedWrapped },
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampUnreadable"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "RED forged expression (a GitHub expression member)",
      subject: SubjectForged { form: ForgedExpression },
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampUnreadable"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "CONTROL quoted scalar is the same stamp (no false refusal)",
      subject: SubjectQuotedScalar,
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "0",
      expected_finding: "paired"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "RED forged truncated (15 digits)",
      subject: SubjectForged { form: ForgedTruncated },
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampUnreadable"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "RED forged extended (17 digits)",
      subject: SubjectForged { form: ForgedExtended },
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampUnreadable"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "RED forged uppercased (hex, wrong case)",
      subject: SubjectForged { form: ForgedUppercased },
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampUnreadable"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "RED forged duplicate member (declared twice)",
      subject: SubjectForged { form: ForgedDuplicate },
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampUnreadable"
    },
    // THE SECOND MEMBER, IN SPELLINGS THE OLD RECOGNISER COULD NOT SEE. Executed against the previous
    // emitted program, both exited 0 reporting a pair: the counter required the literal prefix
    // `  KEY: `, and neither of these lines has it. So the guard certified a workflow that declared the
    // contract stamp twice with two different claims about it -- which is the whole reason a duplicate is
    // refused. They are here rather than only as unit cases because they must be proven against the bytes
    // that ship, the same reason the seven forms above are.
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "RED forged second member, empty value",
      subject: SubjectForged { form: ForgedSecondEmpty },
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampUnreadable"
    },
    CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase {
      label: "RED forged second member, quoted key",
      subject: SubjectForged { form: ForgedSecondQuotedKey },
      running: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe,
      expected_exit: "1",
      expected_finding: "StampUnreadable"
    }
  ]
}

// THE RUNNING SIDE AS A PROBE, never a literal copy of the stamp. Cases that must agree read the
// value the generated workflow actually declares; if that member is missing or malformed, those
// cases cannot silently compare against a stale constant and pass.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_running_probe: String = "__FROM_WORKFLOW__"

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_case_is_red(c: CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase) -> Bool {
  c.expected_exit == "1"
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_case_proceeds(c: CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase) -> Bool {
  c.expected_exit == "0"
}

// THE DISCRIMINATING PAIR, derived rather than asserted. A guard that refuses EVERYTHING passes any
// table of "must refuse" rows -- which is what the fused `-v key=` render did while it was dead. The
// table is therefore only evidence if it holds at least one refusal AND at least one case that must
// PROCEED; this is what the emitted step refuses on, so a future edit that drops every control reds
// the GATE, not a reviewer's attention.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_table_discriminates() -> Bool {
  let cases = compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_cases()
  let reds = filter(cases, compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_case_is_red)
  let proceeds = filter(cases, compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_case_proceeds)
  0 < length(reds) && 0 < length(proceeds)
}

// ONE RED AND ONLY ONE. Two RED rows against the same fixture would double the cost without doubling
// the information, and four rows copied from four heads would suggest the heads differ in workflow
// text when they do not.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_subject_is_fixture(s: CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceSubject) -> Bool {
  match s {
    SubjectFixture => true
    SubjectCommitted => false
    SubjectRewritten => false
    SubjectDemoted => false
    // A QUOTED SCALAR IS NOT THE RECORDED HEAD either -- it is built from the committed artifact by
    // quoting the member, so it is a control over the reader, not the specimen the failure mode records.
    SubjectQuotedScalar => false
    // A forged tree is not the recorded silent-green head either. That the typechecker refuses when a
    // new subject arrives without this arm is the point of a closed union: the earlier match would
    // have kept compiling and quietly decided every forged case was, or was not, the fixture.
    SubjectForged { form: _ } => false
  }
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_case_count_is_declared() -> Bool {
  1 == length(filter(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_cases(),
    c => compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_subject_is_fixture(s: c.subject)))
}

data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_step_name: String = "Execute the contract-stamp guard against the recorded silent-green subject"

data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_harness_path: String = "test/compiler_gate_contract_stamp/run_evidence.sh"

// THE CARRIER AND ITS DISSOLUTION. Same convention as the guard's own scaffold row and as the
// fleet's "Isolate toolchain homes" step: a Scaffold bound by DeclarationRef to the thing that
// produces the shell, and a trigger named as a CAPABILITY rather than a file, a date, or a count.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_shell_emit_scaffold: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_contract_stamp",
    decl_name: "compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_statements",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_shell_emit_dissolution_trigger: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "dissolve-on: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_statements -- bash-node carrier that invokes the contract-stamp evidence harness, one command node per modelled case row; dissolves on either of two capabilities, naming different halves: orch-emitted node kinds in v2.extdeps.languages.bash_build for extraction, structural scanning and argv driving (which retire the residual SHELL), or gunbc.rung_drop v1_required_lanes_withdrawn's restoration_trigger, which retires the residual TEST PROCESS by making these rows a `gunbc test` row evaluated on the required path.")

data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_emit_refused_poison: String = "__GUNBC_ORCH_EMIT_REFUSED__ gunbc contract stamp evidence emission was rejected by v2.workflow.bash_emit: the case table's bash node tree could not be rendered. This literal must never reach a committed workflow; the generation gate refuses instead."

// THE STEP AS NODES, not as joined strings, and one COMMAND NODE PER MODELLED CASE -- so the table
// in this module is what the COMMITTED WORKFLOW CARRIES, not something the shell re-decides. The
// renderable predicate below refuses generation if any modelled label is missing from the emitted
// bytes, so a row that exists only in the model or only in the runner stops the workflow generating.
// That is construction over validation (DESIGN section 5): the emitted script is required to agree
// with the model, rather than being checked against it afterwards.
//
// `set -e` is OFF (set_e: false) deliberately: every case must RUN even when an earlier one fails,
// because a partial table is not evidence, and the step must be able to read the transcript before
// deciding. Under -e the first failing case would abort the step with no diagnostic -- the
// silent-green shape, one level up.
//
// ONLY SHAPES THE GUARD ALREADY PROVES ARE SERIALIZABLE are used: command words, test_bracket, if,
// printf, exit. In particular the final refusal is `[ -e marker ]` rather than `if ! grep ...`,
// because bash_fold_serialize_negation_inner admits only or_else / and_then / pipe / negation, so an
// `if ! <command>` cond has no node representation -- the same constraint that shaped the guard's
// capture. The verdict is therefore carried by a MARKER FILE the harness creates on any failure.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_statements() -> List<Node> {
  let case_nodes = list_map(
    compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_cases(),
    compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_case_command
  )
  // FIRST STATEMENT, and it is the reason a reused runner cannot inherit someone else's verdict:
  // the marker lives in /tmp, which survives between steps on one runner. Clearing it here means the
  // step's own `[ -e marker ]` speaks only about THIS run.
  let prepare = [
    bash_build_command_with_words(
      words: [
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "bash"),
        bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_harness_path),
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "--prepare"),
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "--fail-marker"),
        bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_FAIL"),
        // THE TRANSCRIPT IS CLEARED TOO, not just the marker. /tmp survives between steps on a reused
        // runner, so a transcript left by an earlier run would make the count below reach the modelled
        // number even if THIS run executed nothing -- a third fail-open of the same shape, found while
        // auditing the first two.
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "--transcript"),
        bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_LOG")
      ]
    )
  ]
  // THE STEPS OWNS ITS OWN COMPLETENESS. Two fail-opens existed in the first version of this step,
  // both discovered by RUNNING the emitted bytes against a tampered tree, and both are this class's
  // exact shape -- green while checking nothing:
  //
  //   * if the harness file is DELETED, `bash <path>` fails, and with `set_e: false` the step carries
  //     on to the marker check, finds no marker, and EXITS 0. Verified: `bash step.sh` -> 0.
  //   * if `--prepare` crashes, the same thing happens: nothing checked anything, and nothing checked
  //     that anything checked anything. Verified: a harness whose --prepare exits 3 -> step 0.
  //
  // Checking file EXISTENCE would not be enough either: a harness can exit 0 without running every
  // case. So the step counts the per-case lines the harness printed and refuses unless the number
  // EQUALS the modelled table -- which means a row that vanishes from either side reddens the gate.
  // Only shapes the guard already proves serializable are used (assign from cmdsubst over an or_else,
  // test_bracket, if, printf, exit); `if ! <command>` still has no node form, which is why the
  // comparison is != against the modelled count rather than a negated grep.
  // A PER-RUN TOKEN is what makes the count meaningful. Counting the modelled token alone is NOT
  // enough: my own simulation of the emitted step reproduced the hole -- with the harness DELETED, the
  // step still exited 0, because a transcript left behind by an earlier invocation already held the
  // expected number of modelled-token lines. A stale file satisfies a count of a CONSTANT; it cannot
  // satisfy a count of a value that only exists in THIS run. Built from var words so it expands (a
  // literal word would be single-quoted by the serializer and stay literal).
  let run_token = [
    bash_build_assign(
      name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_RUN_TOKEN",
      value: bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(
        parts: [
          bash_build_word_var(name: "GITHUB_RUN_ID"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "-"),
          bash_build_word_var(name: "GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT")
        ]
      )
    ),

    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(
        words: [
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "-z"),
          bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_RUN_TOKEN")
        ]
      ),
      then_stmts: [
        bash_build_command_with_words(
          words: [
            bash_build_word_lit(text: "printf"),
            bash_build_word_lit(text: "%s\n"),
            bash_build_word_lit(
              text: join(
                [
                  "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp evidence: no run token (GITHUB_RUN_ID/GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT ",
                  "unset), so neither the transcript count nor the fail marker could tell this run from ",
                  "a stale one."
                ],
                ""
              )
            )
          ]
        ),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ]
    ),
    // THE VERDICT CHANNELS ARE PER-RUN. The case count is keyed to the run identity; a channel that is
    // not keyed to it can be cleared by a neighbour. Concurrent runner slots share a host -- this very
    // job carries an "Isolate toolchain homes" step because "concurrent runner slots stop sharing one
    // toolchain" -- so a fixed marker name lets one slot's --prepare delete another slot's failure and
    // turn a real refusal green. A silent widen is a hard reject under DESIGN 5.
    //
    // RUNNER_TEMP is Actions' per-job scratch and GITHUB_RUN_ID-GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT makes it
    // per-ATTEMPT, because a retried job reuses RUNNER_TEMP. Values are built from var words so the
    // serializer renders "$RUNNER_TEMP"'/name' (the fleet-converge axis block is the golden) rather
    // than a single-quoted literal that could never expand.
    bash_build_assign(
      name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_DIR",
      value: bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(
        parts: [
          bash_build_word_var(name: "RUNNER_TEMP"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "/gunbc-contract-stamp-"),
          bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_RUN_TOKEN")
        ]
      )
    ),
    // NO RUNNER_TEMP, NO SHARED ROOT. If RUNNER_TEMP were empty the concatenation above would be
    // "/gunbc-contract-stamp-<token>" -- still unique per run, but written into the filesystem ROOT by
    // a step that is not entitled to write there, and it would fail as a permission error rather than
    // as a located refusal. Refuse on the empty value instead of discovering it through the wrong
    // diagnostic.
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(
        words: [
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "-z"),
          bash_build_word_var(name: "RUNNER_TEMP")
        ]
      ),
      then_stmts: [
        bash_build_command_with_words(
          words: [
            bash_build_word_lit(text: "printf"),
            bash_build_word_lit(text: "%s\n"),
            bash_build_word_lit(
              text: join(
                [
                  "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp evidence: RUNNER_TEMP is unset, so the per-run ",
                  "transcript and fail marker would have no home."
                ],
                ""
              )
            )
          ]
        ),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ]
    ),
    bash_build_command_with_words(
      words: [
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "mkdir"),
        bash_build_word_lit(text: "-p"),
        bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_DIR")
      ]
    ),
    // THE TWO CHANNELS ARE DERIVED FROM THE DIRECTORY, AND ASSIGNED -- not merely named. Every variable
    // the step references must be set by a statement in the step: an earlier revision referenced
    // GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_RUN_TOKEN without ever assigning it, and the omission survived a green
    // witness claim because no claim runs on the required path. run_evidence.sh now refuses if any of
    // these three is referenced without an assignment, so this class cannot ship again unnoticed.
    bash_build_assign(
      name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_LOG",
      value: bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(
        parts: [
          bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_DIR"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "/"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_transcript_name)
        ]
      )
    ),
    bash_build_assign(
      name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_FAIL",
      value: bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(
        parts: [
          bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_DIR"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "/"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_fail_marker_name)
        ]
      )
    )
  ]
  let count_check = [
    bash_build_assign(
      name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_CASES_RAN",
      value: bash_build_word_cmdsubst(
        body: bash_build_or_else(
          left: bash_build_command_with_words(
            words: [
              bash_build_word_lit(text: "grep"),
              bash_build_word_lit(text: "-c"),
              bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_RUN_TOKEN"),
              bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_LOG")
            ]
          ),
          right: bash_build_command_from_lits(lits: ["true"])
        )
      )
    ),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(
        words: [
          // A WORD VAR, not a literal: the serializer single-quotes literals, which would compare the
          // NAME as text and never expand it. Var words render as "$NAME". The golden at
          // instrument-dispatch.yml:46 shows the same shape ('[' "$machine" '-lt' "$X" ']').
          bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_CASES_RAN"),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "!="),
          bash_build_word_lit(text: to_string(length(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_cases())))
        ]
      ),
      then_stmts: [
        bash_build_command_with_words(
          words: [
            bash_build_word_lit(text: "printf"),
            bash_build_word_lit(text: "%s\n"),
            bash_build_word_lit(
              text: join(
                [
                  "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp evidence: the harness reported ",
                  compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_transcript_marker,
                  " case lines, expected ",
                  to_string(length(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_cases())),
                  " modelled cases FOR THIS RUN -- the harness is missing, crashed, or ran a table that is not the model's. See $",
                  compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_transcript_var
                ],
                ""
              )
            )
          ]
        ),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ]
    )
  ]
  let summary = [
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(
        words: [
          bash_build_word_lit(text: "-e"),
          bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_FAIL")
        ]
      ),
      then_stmts: [
        bash_build_command_with_words(
          words: [
            bash_build_word_lit(text: "printf"),
            bash_build_word_lit(text: "%s\n"),
            bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_refusal)
          ]
        ),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ]
    )
  ]
  // THE TOKEN ASSIGNMENT IS FIRST, before any case can be handed it and before the count can read it.
  // It was once defined and never appended -- see the commit: the step then referenced a variable no
  // statement had set, and its own -z refusal fired. That refusal is what caught the omission, because
  // the claim that should have caught it never runs on the required path.
  list_append(
    left: run_token,
    right: list_append(
      left: prepare,
      right: list_append(left: case_nodes, right: list_append(left: count_check, right: summary))
    )
  )
}

// ONE CASE INVOCATION: `bash <harness> --case <label> <subject> <running> <expected-exit> <finding>`.
// Every argument is a WORD, so quoting is the serializer's problem and not ours: nothing here is
// spliced into shell syntax, and a label containing parentheses or spaces survives as data.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_case_command(c: CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceCase) -> Node {
  bash_build_command_with_words(
    words: [
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "bash"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_harness_path),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "--case"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: c.label),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_subject_text(s: c.subject)),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: c.running),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: c.expected_exit),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: c.expected_finding),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "--transcript"),
      bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_LOG"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "--fail-marker"),
      bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_FAIL"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "--marker"),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_transcript_marker),
      bash_build_word_lit(text: "--run-token"),
      bash_build_word_var(name: "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_RUN_TOKEN")
    ]
  )
}

// THE SUBJECT SYMBOL AS DATA, not as a path. The runner owns the mechanical step of turning a symbol
// into a file (copying the committed artifact, moving the stamp member, demoting it into a step-level
// env); the MODEL owns which subjects exist and what each one is supposed to prove. A path in the
// model would be a second spelling of the same fact, free to disagree in silence.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_subject_text(s: CompilerGateContractStampEvidenceSubject) -> String {
  match s {
    SubjectFixture => "fixture"
    SubjectCommitted => "committed"
    SubjectRewritten => "rewritten"
    SubjectDemoted => "demoted"
    SubjectQuotedScalar => "quoted-scalar"
    SubjectForged { form: f } => "forged:" + compiler_gate_contract_stamp_forged_form_text(f: f)
  }
}

// THE FORM NAME, likewise as data. The harness matches on this string and refuses an unknown one, so a
// form added to the type without a harness arm fails loudly instead of being skipped.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_forged_form_text(f: CompilerGateContractStampForgedForm) -> String {
  match f {
    ForgedWrapped => "wrapped"
    ForgedExpression => "expression"
    ForgedTruncated => "truncated"
    ForgedExtended => "extended"
    ForgedUppercased => "uppercased"
    ForgedDuplicate => "duplicate"
    ForgedSecondEmpty => "second-empty"
    ForgedSecondQuotedKey => "second-quoted-key"
  }
}

// THE PATHS ARE PER-RUN, AND THEY ARE VAR NAMES RATHER THAN LITERAL PATHS.
// Review 82230 (claude/opus[1m], REQUEST_CHANGES) filed as blocking that both files sat at FIXED /tmp
// names while this very job carries an "Isolate toolchain homes" step because "concurrent runner slots
// stop sharing one toolchain". The run token protected the case COUNT; it did not protect the MARKER.
// Slot A leaves a failure marker, slot B's --prepare deletes it before A reads it, and A exits green
// with a failed case -- a silent widen, which DESIGN 5 rejects outright. Run the interleaving the other
// way and a stale marker from another slot reddens a clean run. So each run owns a directory keyed by
// the same run identity the count is keyed to: one identity, both channels, no shared path.
//
// They are modelled as VAR NAMES because the value must be built at run time from RUNNER_TEMP and the
// run identity, and the serializer single-quotes literal words -- a literal here could never expand.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_dir_var: String = "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_DIR"

data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_transcript_var: String = "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_LOG"

data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_fail_marker_var: String = "GUNBC_CONTRACT_STAMP_EVIDENCE_FAIL"

// The file names inside that directory. Fixed, because the directory is already unique per run; these
// are how the harness and the step agree on which two files of that directory they mean.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_transcript_name: String = "gunbc-contract-stamp-evidence.log"

data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_fail_marker_name: String = "gunbc-contract-stamp-evidence.fail"

// The harness creates this on ANY failing case. The step's refusal reads its EXISTENCE rather than
// parsing its own verdict prose, so a harness that crashes mid-case -- or is deleted, or is never
// enrolled -- still leaves a marker behind: absence of the marker is only green if the harness got
// far enough to remove a stale one, which it does as its first act.
// Every modelled case prints exactly one line beginning with this token, through the harness. It is
// a MODEL value: the step counts occurrences of it and the harness prints it, so neither side can
// quietly shrink the table.
// THE COUNTED TOKEN IS THE MODEL'S, handed to the harness as argv, so the harness prints exactly what
// the step greps for: one spelling, not two that can drift. It is long and distinctive because
// grep -c counts LINES, and the tail of each line carries the guard's own diagnostic -- counting a
// weak token like rc= would let a guard message containing those two characters inflate the tally and
// make a partial run look complete.
data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_transcript_marker: String = "gunbc-contract-stamp-evidence-case"

// (a fixed /tmp path here would be the shared-channel defect; see the var rows above)

data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_verdict_ok: String = "ALL ASSERTIONS HELD"

data compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_refusal: String = "REFUSED gunbc contract stamp evidence: the harness did not report ALL ASSERTIONS HELD. See its output above."

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_marker_line() -> String {
  concat("# ", concat(dissolution_description(condition: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_shell_emit_dissolution_trigger), "\n"))
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_script_outcome() -> Outcome<String> {
  match bash_emit_stmts(statements: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_statements(), set_e: false) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: ds } => Rejected { diagnostics: ds }
    Accepted { value: body, diagnostics: ds } =>
      Accepted {
        value: concat(compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_marker_line(), body),
        diagnostics: ds
      }
  }
}

// FAIL CLOSED AT GENERATION, in the same shape as the guard's gate predicate: an unrenderable tree
// stops the workflow generating at all, and the marker plus the refusal must SURVIVE rendering, so a
// refactor cannot quietly drop either.
fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_script_is_renderable() -> Bool {
  match compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_script_outcome() {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } =>
      !string_contains(s: script, pattern: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_emit_refused_poison)
        && string_contains(s: script, pattern: "dissolve-on: ")
        && string_contains(s: script, pattern: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_verdict_ok)
        && string_contains(s: script, pattern: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_harness_path)
        && string_contains(s: script, pattern: "exit 1")
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => false
  }
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_script() -> String {
  match compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_script_outcome() {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } => script
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_emit_refused_poison
  }
}

fn compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_step_name },
    id: none,
    run: compiler_gate_contract_stamp_evidence_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}
